      "title": "Defect–Optical Read-Across for Discovering High-NIR Red Mn/Cr Oxide Pigments",
      "motivation": "The original query asks whether more color pigments can be discovered. The evidence suggests that the answer is yes, but the opportunity is not new perceptual colors; it is new pigment materials with known hues and improved function, stability, or sustainability. Red cool pigments illustrate this frontier: Li2MnO3-based red pigments can combine visible red color with near-infrared reflectance [Gramm et al., 2020](https://doi.org/10.1002/zaac.202000274), Li3AlMnO5-based oxides pursue the same red/NIR objective [Divya et al., 2021](https://doi.org/10.1016/j.ceramint.2021.07.218), La3LiMnO7-type oxides extend Mn-based bright color into orange/red regions [Oka et al., 2021](https://doi.org/10.3390/molecules26206243), and Cr3+-doped LaAlO3 shows that red color can be engineered together with NIR reflectance [Liu et al., 2025](https://doi.org/10.1016/j.ceramint.2025.08.005). However, discovery remains empirical because local coordination, distortion, defect states, and powder scattering are not jointly used to predict final pigment performance [Ransmayr et al., 2022](https://doi.org/10.1103/physrevmaterials.6.105003). The proposed idea imports quantitative read-across structure–property modeling from predictive toxicology, where structurally similar analogues are used to infer unknown endpoints with applicability domains and uncertainty estimates [Banerjee et al., 2024](https://doi.org/10.1080/10408444.2024.2386260). Adapted to pigment science, this mechanism treats known oxide pigments as analogues defined not merely by composition but by local coordination, Mn/Cr oxidation state, polyhedral distortion, vacancy-sensitive optical loss, and scattering-relevant morphology. The primary testable hypothesis is that local-defect/defect-optical similarity read-across predicts the hue and NIR reflectance of unseen Mn/Cr oxide pigments more accurately than composition-only similarity, and that this predictive gain enables discovery of at least one phase-pure, high-NIR red pigment not present in the training library.",
